<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<section xml:id="section-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Taylor Series and its convergence</title>
  <introduction>
    <p>
      The main objectives of this section are to answer the following questions:
    </p>
  </introduction>
  <objectives>
      <ol>
        <li>
          <p>
            Under what conditions, the function <m> f(x) </m> is equal to the Taylor series generated 
            by <m>f</m> at <m>x = a</m>? That is, when can we say 
            <m>f(x) = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(a)}{n!} (x-a)^n</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            If the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m>, <m>p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k</m> , is used as an 
            approximation to <m>f(x)</m>, what is the error in this approximation? 
            That is, what is the value of <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>?
          </p>
        </li>
      </ol>

  </objectives>

   <subsection xml:id="subsection-1">
    <title> The Remainder Theorem </title>
    <theorem xml:id="thm-remainder-theorem">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval 
          <m>I</m> containing <m>a</m>. Then for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number 
          <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
          <me>
            f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
          </me>
          where 
          <me>
            p_n(x)= \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
          </me>
          and
        <me>
          R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
        </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
    </theorem>
    <lemma xml:id="lemma-mean-value-theorem">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is (n+1) times differentiable. Also, suppose that   
        </p>
        
      </statement>
      </lemma>
    <proof>
      <p>
        The proof of the remainder theorem is based on the mean value theorem and Taylor's theorem.
      </p>
    </proof>
    <remark>
  <title>The existence of <m>c</m></title>
  <p>
   The remainder theorem, merely points out the existence of the number <m>c</m>. It does not provide 
   a method for finding the value of <m>c</m>. However, in many cases, we can find <alert>an upper bound</alert> 
   for the error term <m>R_n(x)</m> without knowing the exact value of <m>c</m>. 
  </p>
</remark>
<example>
      <title>Finding an upper bound for the error term</title>
      <p>
        Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. We want to approximate <m>e^{0.1}</m> 
        using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>. The Taylor polynomial is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_2(x) = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2}
      </me>
      <p>
        The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.1) = \frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!} (0.1)^3= \frac{e^c}{6} (0.1)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>c</m> is between <m>0</m> and <m>0.1</m>, we have <m>0 \leq c \leq 0.1</m>. 
        Therefore, we can bound the remainder term as follows:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.1) \leq \frac{e^{0.1}}{6} (0.1)^3 \approx 0.000184
      </me>
      <p>
        This shows that the error in approximating <m>e^{0.1}</m> using the Taylor polynomial 
        of degree 2 is less than <m>0.000184</m>.
      </p>
      <p>
      In fact, if we compute the actual value of <m>e^{0.1}</m> and compare it with the approximation
      given by <m>p_2(0.1)</m>, we find that the actual error is approximately <m>0.000</m>, which 
      is indeed less than the upper bound we calculated.
      </p>
      </example>
  </subsection>
 
    <subsection xml:id="subsection-2">
    <title>An example of a Taylor series that converges</title>
    <p>
      Consider the function <m>f(x) = e^x</m>. We know that <m>f^{(n)}(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>n\geq 0</m>. 
      Therefore, the Taylor series generated by <m>f</m> at <m>x=0</m> is given by:
    </p> but it can be found in many calculus textbooks.
    <me>
      \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{f^{(n)}(0)}{n!} x^n = \sum_{n=0}^{\infty} \frac{1}{n!} x^n
    </me>
    <p>
      We will show that this series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x\in \R</m>. To do this, we will use the remainder theorem. We have:
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1} = \frac{e^c}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      First, we consider the case when <m>x>0</m>. Since <m>c</m> is between <m>0</m> and <m>x</m>, 
      we have <m>0 \leq c \leq x</m>. Therefore, we can bound the remainder term as follows:    
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) \leq \frac{e^x}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      Now we can compute the limit of the remainder term as <m>n\to \infty</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \lim_{n\to \infty} |R_n(x)| \leq \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{e^x}{(n+1)!} x^{n+1} = 0
    </me>
    <p>
      This shows that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x>0</m>. 
      The second case is when <m>x &lt; 0</m>. In this case, since <m>c</m> is between <m>x</m> and <m>0</m>,
      we have <m>x \leq c \leq 0</m>. Hence, we can bound the remainder term as follows:
    </p>
    <me>
      |R_n(x)| \leq \frac{1}{(n+1)!} |x|^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      Now we can compute the limit of the remainder term as <m>n\to \infty</m>:
    </p>
    <me>
      \lim_{n\to \infty} |R_n(x)| \leq \lim_{n\to \infty} \frac{1}{(n+1)!} |x|^{n+1} = 0
    </me>
    <p>
      This shows that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x &lt; 0</m>. Therefore, 
      we conclude that the Taylor series converges to <m>f(x) = e^x</m> for all <m>x\in \R</m>.
    </p>    
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsection-3">
    <title> The error in using a Taylor polynomial</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will discuss how to approximate the error in using a Taylor polynomial to approximate a function. 
      We will use the remainder theorem to bound the error term <m>R_n(x)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Suppose we want to approximate <m>f(x)</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree <m>n</m> at <m>x=a</m>. 
      The error in this approximation is given by <m>R_n(x) = f(x) - p_n(x)</m>. By the remainder theorem, we have:
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}
    </me>
    <p>
      To bound the error term, we need to find an upper bound for <m>|f^{(n+1)}(c)|</m> on the interval between 
      <m>a</m> and <m>x</m>.
    </p> 
    <example>
      <title>Approximating <m>e^x</m> using a Taylor polynomial</title>
      <p>
        Let's approximate <m>f(x) = e^x</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 at <m>x=0</m>. 
        The Taylor polynomial is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        p_2(x) = 1 + x + \frac{x^2}{2}
      </me>
      <p>
        We want to approximate <m>e^{0.5}</m>. The error term is given by:
      </p>
      <me>
        R_2(0.5) = \frac{f^{(3)}(c)}{3!} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(3)}(x) = e^x</m>, we have:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_2(0.5)| \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.5)^3
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, the error in approximating <m>e^{0.5}</m> using the Taylor polynomial of degree 2 is bounded by:
      </p>
      <me>
        |R_2(0.5)| \leq \frac{e^{0.5}}{6} (0.125) \approx 0.034
      </me>
    </example>

    <example> 
      <title>Approximating <m>\sin(x)</m> using a Taylor polynomial</title>
      <p>
        Assume that we use the Taylor polynomial of degree 3 at <m>x=0</m> to approximate <m>\sin(x)</m>. For approximately what values of <m>x</m> 
        can you replace <m>\sin(x)</m> by such a Taylor polynomial with an error of magnitude no greater than <m> 4 \times 10^{-3}</m>?
      </p>
    </example>  
  </subsection>

  <subsection xml:id="subsection-5">
    <title> Proof of the remainder theorem</title>
    <p>
      In this subsection, we will provide a proof of the remainder theorem. 
      The proof is based on the mean value theorem and Taylor's theorem. 
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>f</m> be a function that has <m>n+1</m> continuous derivatives on an open interval
       <m>I</m> containing <m>a </m>. We want to show that for each <m>x\in I</m> and for each 
       positive integer <m>n</m>, there exists a number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that
    </p>
    <me>
      f(x) = p_n(x) + R_n(x),
    </me>
    <p>
      where
    </p>
    <me>
      p_n(x) = \sum_{k=0}^{n} \frac{f^{(k)}(a)}{k!} (x-a)^k,
    </me>
    <p>
      and
    </p>
    <me>
      R_n(x) = \frac{f^{(n+1)}(c)}{(n+1)!} (x-a)^{n+1}.
    </me>
    <p>
      To prove this, we will first prove the following lemma:
    </p>
    <lemma xml:id="lemma-1">
      <statement>   
        <p>
          Let <m>f(x)</m> be a function that is <m>(n+1)</m>-times differentiable. 
          Also, suppose that <m>f(a) = f'(a) = f''(a) = ... = f^{(n)}(a) = 0</m>. 
          Then there exists a number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
          <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement> 
    </lemma>
    <proof> 
      <p>
        The proof of this lemma is based on the mean value theorem. 
        Since <m>f(a) = f'(a) = f''(a) = ... = f^{(n)}(a) = 0</m>, we can apply the mean value theorem 
        repeatedly to show that there exists a number <m>c</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
        <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = 0</m>. Below we will do so step by step. 
      </p>
      <p>
        First, since <m>f(a) = f(x) = 0</m>, by the mean value theorem, there exists a number <m>c_1</m> between 
        <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f'(c_1) = \frac{f(x) - f(a)}{x - a} = 0
      </me>
      <p>
       Second, since <m>f'(a) = f'(c_1) = 0</m>, by the mean value theorem, there exists a number <m>c_2</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>c_1</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f''(c_2) = \frac{f'(c_1) - f'(a)}{c_1 - a} = 0
      </me>
      <p>
        Then by continuing this process, we can show that there exists a number 
        <m>c_n</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>c_{n-1}</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f^{(n)}(c_n) = 0
      </me>
      <p>
        Since <m>f^{(n)}(a) = f^{(n)}(c_n) = 0</m>, by the mean value theorem, there exists a number <m>c_{n+1}</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>c_n</m> such that 
      </p>
      <me>
        f^{(n+1)}(c_{n+1}) = 0
      </me>
      <p>
        Therefore, we have shown that there exists a number <m>c = c_{n+1}</m> between <m>a</m> and <m>x</m> such that 
        <m>f^{(n+1)}(c) = 0</m>.
      </p>
    </proof>

    
    <p>
      This completes the proof of the remainder theorem.
    </p>
  </subsection>
    

    
  

</section>